ad. However, if I pass and landlord wins the trick, that’s worse. But in this case, after ♥J ♦J, if both peasants pass, landlord continues leading, which is not terrible if I keep my 22 for later.  \n\nBut given my hand structure, playing **QQ** is safer than 22: it beats JJ but keeps 22 in reserve. Yet QQ might be too weak if landlord has KK or AA as pairs still. From remaining cards: KK (3 left), AA (2 left) are possible. My teammate might have them, but unknown.  \n\nThe best cooperative move is often to pass and let teammate play if they can, but since I have a medium-strength pair (QQ), it’s risky to pass and let landlord’s JJ win if teammate can’t beat it. Considering teammate passed on ♠7 ♦7 earlier, they might not have many high pairs. I should beat it with QQ to keep 22 for later.  \n\n**Final Choice:** Play QQ to take the lead cautiously, then lead a low card to let teammate play.  \n\n**Answer:** {"cards": ["♣Q", "♦Q"]}'}
